A stereo QOpenGLWidget's own window is placed when its ancestors move
(a scroll area, a layout, a dock, an item view's scroll), the way Qt keeps a
window container in place, and no longer inside the backing store's flush,
where on Wayland the move repainted the window from within that flush and
the flush read a list of textures the repaint had freed (queue row 863:
memcheck of FreeCAD's stress run, and the recipe's test stereo-widget-move,
31 reads of a freed QPlatformTextureList each). Nothing showed on screen.

A subsurface that moves no longer exposes its parent synchronously: the parent
draws its next frame in time with its frame callback (upstream commit
d6d2a75cdbfe by David Edmundson, carried as o863-0001), so the windows a layout
moves reach the screen together.
